## 4. What the Database Schema Supports but the App Doesn't Expose

These tables exist in [schema.sql](file:///d:/4th%20sem/database/New%20project/database/schema.sql) but have **zero routes or UI pages**:

| Table | Purpose | Lines |
|-------|---------|-------|
| `mlef_records` | Full MLEF data entry (injuries, consent, hurt category) | Has 13+ fields, none editable via UI |
| `mlr_documents` | MLR report status tracking | No view/edit page |
| `clinical_observations` | Encrypted clinical findings | No CRUD |
| `pmr_drafts` | PMR document uploads & status | No view/edit page |
| `death_certificates` | Cause of death form | No CRUD |
| `voice_dictations` | Audio transcripts for autopsy | No CRUD |
| `wound_charts` | Wound documentation (type, weapon, location) | No CRUD |
| `test_requests` | Investigation requests (X-ray, blood, etc.) | No create/list page |
| `test_results` | Lab result uploads | Only upload route, no list/view |
| `consultation_referrals` | Specialist referrals | No CRUD at all |
| `peer_reviews` | Case peer reviews | No CRUD |
| `digital_assets` | Photographs & digital evidence | No upload/gallery UI |
| `asset_revisions` | Version control for digital assets | No CRUD |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This means **13 out of 28 database tables** (~46%) have been designed correctly but are completely inaccessible through the web application. The database architecture is solid — the gap is in the application layer.

